Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where CMS content becomes immutable — it wraps defaultContent in useMemo and never updates it. All 236+ components via useContent → useContentstackContext read from this frozen context. Live preview requires this provider to accept and reactively apply content updates from the Contentstack preview channel, which it currently cannot do.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo block (lines 56-61) with useState to hold content, initialized from defaultContent. Add a useEffect that subscribes to Contentstack live-preview updates (via @contentstack/live-preview SDK's onContentUpdate callback or polling) and calls the state setter to push new content into the context, triggering re-renders across all useContent consumers. Also add a preview flag to IContentStackProviderProps so this subscription is only active in CMS preview mode. In src/services/contentstack.ts, the Stack initialization (line 69) must also be updated to pass the preview host and preview token when in preview mode — the current options object (lines 55-67) only configures delivery token and production host, with no preview support.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a content editor saves a change to page content in the CMS, the preview page updates to display the new content automatically, without the editor manually refreshing the browser.
- The preview page displays the edited content as fully rendered page output — styled headings, body text, links, and images in their page layout — rather than raw field values, placeholder text, or unstyled markup.
- On the GO Transit branded preview page, when an editor saves a change to GO Transit content in the CMS, the preview updates automatically to show the new content with GO Transit branding and styling, without a manual refresh.
- On the UP Express branded preview page, when an editor saves a change to UP Express content in the CMS, the preview updates automatically to show the new content with UP Express branding and styling, without a manual refresh.
- On the MX branded preview page, when an editor saves a change to MX content in the CMS, the preview updates automatically to show the new content with MX branding and styling, without a manual refresh.
- When an editor makes and saves several consecutive content changes in the CMS, each saved change appears in the preview page in turn, with the preview always showing the most recently saved version without any manual refresh.

